# Build-context exclusions. There was no such file, which had two consequences.
#
# 1. **The harnesses shipped in the production image.** `COPY server/ ./server/`
#    takes `e2e-*.js` and `run-e2e.js` with it. They are dev tooling: they boot a
#    second app instance on a fixed port, mint API keys and OAuth clients, and
#    write to a database. Nothing in production needs them and nothing in
#    production should be able to run them.
#
# 2. **The host's `node_modules` was uploaded and then discarded.** Both images
#    `COPY server/ ./server/` and immediately overwrite it with
#    `COPY --from=serverdeps …/node_modules`. Hundreds of MB of build context for
#    nothing — and the host's `better-sqlite3` is compiled for the DEVELOPER'S
#    platform, so if that overwrite order is ever changed, a macOS or glibc binary
#    lands in an Alpine image and fails at runtime rather than at build time.
#
# ── What must NOT be excluded, and why it is easy to get wrong ────────────────
#
# `web/scripts/` stays. Stage 1 runs `npm run build`, which runs `check:ui` from
# there (loading states, table grids, charts). Excluding it breaks the image
# build — I did exactly that on the first attempt at this file, which is why the
# `image` job in ci.yml exists and why this version is gated by it rather than
# discovered on the production host mid-deploy.
#
# Everything the two Dockerfiles actually consume: web/ (whole tree, built in
# stage 1), server/ (minus the harnesses), marketing/ (cloud only), agent/, and
# both package-lock.json files for `npm ci`.
#
# Note `unbound` builds from its own context (`build: ./unbound`), so none of
# this applies to it.

.git
.github
.gitignore

# uploaded, then overwritten by the serverdeps stage — pure waste, and a
# cross-platform native binary waiting to be copied into the wrong image
node_modules
**/node_modules

# dev-only tooling: never part of a running instance
server/e2e-*.js
server/run-e2e.js
scripts/

# built inside stage 1; a stale local copy must never shadow it
web/dist

# not consumed by any Dockerfile. Named explicitly rather than as `*.md`: that
# glob is root-only under Docker's matcher (patterns do not cross `/`) but NOT
# under several tools that read this file, and `agent/README.md` ships with the
# host-agent files served at /agent. Three names cost nothing; relying on a
# subtle matcher rule to protect a fourth file costs a debugging session.
docs/
CLAUDE.md
README.md
CONTRIBUTING.md

# never in an image. .gitignore already keeps them out of the repo, but the
# build context is the working tree, not the index — an uncommitted .env would
# otherwise be uploaded.
.env
.env.*
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
